Set the tunnel's settings: the public address your devices dial,
the network they get addresses on, and which port to listen on.

The endpoint is the one thing olr cannot work out for itself. If a
name already tracks this box's address, use it — `olr dial show`
lists the ones olr keeps current.

Changing the network renumbers every device, so every configuration
already on a phone stops working. olr reports that as a disruptive
change; `--dry-run` shows it before you commit to it.

Usage:
  olr remote set [flags]

Flags:
      --address string              this box's own address on that network
      --endpoint string             public name or address your devices dial, optionally with a port
  -h, --help                        help for set
      --interface string            kernel interface name for the tunnel (default wg0)
      --no-address                  use the first address in the network, which is the default
      --no-raw-wireguard-conf       remove the extra WireGuard configuration
      --port uint16                 UDP port to listen on (default 51820)
      --raw-wireguard-conf string   extra WireGuard configuration, appended verbatim
      --subnet string               network dial-in devices get addresses on, e.g. 10.6.0.0/24

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
